From: jaydens-tasty-slop
 <325336397+jaydens-tasty-slop@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 10:26:57 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] drm/mipi-dsi: add a flag for a clock lane started after the
 peripheral reset

A DSI host starts the clock lane in pre_enable: in HS for a continuous
clock, in LP-11 with MIPI_DSI_CLOCK_NON_CONTINUOUS. A peripheral that
sends commands from its own pre_enable sets pre_enable_prev_first, so it
releases its reset with the clock lane already in HS.

Some display controllers do not accept that. The Samsung ANA38401 DDI
(AMS968HH01 panel in the Galaxy Tab S3) ignores every command and read
when the HS clock was running through its reset, and it does not answer
with a non-continuous clock either. It works when the lanes are in LP-11
through the reset and the continuous HS clock starts after it, which is
the order the vendor display driver uses for this panel (qcom,mdss-dsi-
lp11-init and qcom,mdss-dsi-force-clock-lane-hs: mdss_dsi_on() in
drivers/video/msm/mdss/mdss_dsi.c releases the panel reset with the lanes
in LP-11, waits qcom,mdss-dsi-init-delay-us, then sets the clock lane's
HS request; Samsung's GPL-2.0 kernel source for the SM-T820).

Add MIPI_DSI_CLOCK_DEFERRED_HS for such peripherals: the host keeps the
clock lane in LP-11 in pre_enable and starts the continuous HS clock at
the first transfer, or in enable if the peripheral sent nothing before.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/drm_bridge.c | 7 +++++++
 include/drm/drm_mipi_dsi.h   | 2 ++
 2 files changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_bridge.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_bridge.c
index a52d5f5b9f..0c9ed0bb73 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_bridge.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_bridge.c
@@ -173,6 +173,13 @@
  * clock lane to either LP-11 or HS depending on the mode_flag
  * %MIPI_DSI_CLOCK_NON_CONTINUOUS.
  *
+ * Some DSI peripherals need to see the clock lane leave LP-11 after their
+ * reset, which they release in their own pre_enable. Such a peripheral sets
+ * %MIPI_DSI_CLOCK_DEFERRED_HS together with &pre_enable_prev_first: the host
+ * then leaves the clock lane in LP-11 in pre_enable and starts the continuous
+ * HS clock at the first transfer, or in its enable operation if no transfer
+ * came first.
+ *
  * Ordinarily the downstream bridge DSI peripheral pre_enable will have been
  * called before the DSI host. If the DSI peripheral requires LP-11 and/or
  * the clock lane to be in HS mode prior to pre_enable, then it can set the
diff --git a/include/drm/drm_mipi_dsi.h b/include/drm/drm_mipi_dsi.h
index 7ff4396725..0c031da05f 100644
--- a/include/drm/drm_mipi_dsi.h
+++ b/include/drm/drm_mipi_dsi.h
@@ -140,6 +140,8 @@ struct mipi_dsi_host *of_find_mipi_dsi_host_by_node(struct device_node *node);
 #define MIPI_DSI_HS_PKT_END_ALIGNED	BIT(12)
 /* pack all DSC slices for a line into a single packet */
 #define MIPI_DSI_MODE_DSC_ALL_SLICES_IN_PKT	BIT(13)
+/* keep the continuous clock in LP-11 until the first transfer after power-on */
+#define MIPI_DSI_CLOCK_DEFERRED_HS	BIT(14)
 
 enum mipi_dsi_pixel_format {
 	MIPI_DSI_FMT_RGB888,
